„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙDDoth a fountain send forth at the same place sweet water and bitter?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ{Can the fig tree, my brethren, bear olive berries? either a vine, figs? so can no fountain both yield salt water and fresh.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book:§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙWho is a wise man and endued with knowledge among you? let him shew out of a good conversation his works with meekness of wisdom.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙbBut if ye have bitter envying and strife in your hearts, glory not, and lie not against the truth.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙIThis wisdom descendeth not from above, but is earthly, sensual, devilish.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙHFor where envying and strife is, there is confusion and every evil work.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book:§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses’„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¬But the wisdom that is from above is first pure, then peaceable, gentle, and easy to be intreated, full of mercy and good fruits, without partiality, and without hypocrisy.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙHAnd the fruit of righteousness is sown in peace of them that make peace.¦layout ªlast_verse